  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>A slurry or silage store carries three linked risks: structural integrity, pollution control, and lethal gas released on agitation</li><li>Slurry gas, principally hydrogen sulphide, is released within seconds of mixing and can be rapidly fatal to people and livestock; ventilation and keeping everyone clear is the core control</li><li>Stores are governed in England by the SSAFO rules, with equivalents across the UK nations, covering construction, capacity and maintenance</li><li>A structural failure is both a drowning risk and a serious pollution incident, so the structural check protects people and watercourses at once</li><li>Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user per month, dormant users free, so a farm with seasonal and contract labour only pays for who actually works</li></ul></div><h2>What a slurry store inspection is for</h2><p>Slurry and silage stores hold large volumes of material that is corrosive, polluting and, when disturbed, dangerous. A regular inspection has one job: to catch a problem, a hairline crack, a weeping joint, a corroded reception pit, a blocked ventilation route, while it is still cheap and safe to fix, rather than after a wall lets go or someone is overcome by gas.</p><p>Software does not make a store safe. Safe practice does. What software does is make the inspection easy to complete in the yard, hard to forget the gas and ventilation checks, and quick to get in front of the person who can act. A good tool means the check happens on a routine, not only after an incident.</p><h2>Slurry gas: the risk that kills</h2><p>Slurry generates gases as it breaks down, and agitating or mixing it releases them in a sudden burst, especially hydrogen sulphide. High concentrations can overwhelm a person in seconds, and at the highest levels the sense of smell is deadened so there is no warning. Cattle in adjoining buildings have been lost the same way.</p><p>The controls are procedural. Mix only with good natural ventilation, ideally on a breezy day; open the building up; remove all people and animals from the building and area during and after agitation; never enter a store or a space where gas can collect. Quickler can prompt the operator through the safe procedure and record that ventilation was opened and the area cleared before mixing began. It records that steps were followed; it does not detect gas or replace a gas monitor or the HSE guidance.</p><h2>SSAFO and pollution control</h2><p>Slurry stores are regulated for water pollution. In England the relevant rules are commonly known as SSAFO, from the Water Resources (Control of Pollution) (Silage, Slurry and Agricultural Fuel Oil) Regulations, with broadly equivalent regimes in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland. They set standards for construction, minimum storage capacity, and keeping stores maintained and impermeable. Evidence that a store was built to standard, is checked on a schedule, and that defects were repaired is exactly what an environmental regulator wants to see. Requirements differ between nations and change; confirm what applies to your holding.</p><h2>The structural checklist</h2><ul><li><strong>Walls and joints:</strong> cracks, movement, leaks, corrosion</li><li><strong>Liners and membranes:</strong> damage, displacement, seepage</li><li><strong>Reception pit and transfer points:</strong> condition, blockages, integrity</li><li><strong>Covers, guarding and edge protection:</strong> to prevent a fall or drowning</li><li><strong>Ventilation routes:</strong> clear and adequate for safe mixing</li><li><strong>Surrounding ground:</strong> signs of seepage or pollution</li></ul><p>A structural failure empties a store into the environment and creates a drowning hazard, so the structural check protects people and watercourses together. Any significant movement or damage should be assessed by a competent structural engineer; the app records the finding, it does not certify the structure.</p><h2>What to look for in a slurry inspection tool</h2><ul><li><strong>Safe-procedure prompting:</strong> the tool should walk the operator through ventilation and clearing the area before mixing</li><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the check completed in the yard, not reconstructed in the office</li><li><strong>Photo evidence:</strong> a developing crack documented and tracked over time</li><li><strong>Only pay for who works:</strong> charging for every seat punishes a farm with seasonal labour. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user per month, dormant users free, unlimited users</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> date, person, store and every finding, for the HSE inspector or environmental regulator</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler records the inspection and can prompt the safe mixing procedure. It does not detect gas, replace a gas monitor, or certify the structure of a store. Slurry gas kills fast; treat the safe system of work and the HSE farm safety guidance as authoritative, and get a competent structural engineer to any real structural concern. This page is general information, not legal or safety advice. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your farm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Why is slurry gas so dangerous?</h3><p>When slurry is agitated or mixed it releases a sudden burst of gases, principally hydrogen sulphide, which can overcome a person in seconds. At very high concentrations it deadens the sense of smell, removing any warning. It has been fatal to farmers and livestock. The core controls are to mix only with good ventilation, remove all people and animals from the building and area, and never enter a store or space where gas can collect. Follow the current HSE farm safety guidance.</p><h3>What rules cover slurry stores in the UK?</h3><p>Slurry stores are regulated for water pollution. In England the rules are commonly known as SSAFO, from the Water Resources (Control of Pollution) (Silage, Slurry and Agricultural Fuel Oil) Regulations, with equivalent regimes in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland. They cover construction, storage capacity and maintenance. Requirements differ between nations and change; confirm what applies to your holding.</p><h3>How often should a slurry store be inspected?</h3><p>There is no single universal interval, but stores should be inspected on a regular schedule and before high-risk activities such as mixing, with structural condition, guarding, covers and ventilation checked. Any significant structural concern should be assessed by a competent structural engineer. Check the current guidance and any conditions attached to your holding.</p><h3>Can I run slurry store inspections over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's inspection runs over the WhatsApp Business API. Whoever is in the yard receives each item in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with text, a voice note or a photo, and the record generates automatically. It can also prompt the safe mixing procedure. Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the farm's behalf.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/livestock-transport-check-uk.html\">Livestock transport check</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/farm-safety-inspection-app-uk.html\">Farm safety inspection app</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/farm-machinery-inspection-checklist-uk.html\">Farm machinery inspection checklist</a></li></ul>",
